---
/**
 * /about/ — who is fetching what, at what rate, and how to make it stop.
 *
 * The escalation path named here is deliberately the one that does NOT destroy
 * the data: an email or an issue, answered in a commit. If the cheapest thing an
 * annoyed county admin can do is file a GitHub abuse report or block the whole
 * IP range, that is what will happen, and everything already collected becomes
 * unmaintainable. Making the low-friction path the constructive one is the
 * control.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';
import { coverageSummary } from '../lib/deals';
import { withBase } from '../lib/nav';

const sum = coverageSummary();
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What this project collects, how, and how to ask it to stop."
  path="/about/"
  prose
>
  <div class="page wrap">
    <header class="page__head">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>What this is, and what it is not</h1>
      <p class="page__lead">
        A research tool over public property records in {sum.total} Blue Ridge counties across North
        Carolina, Tennessee, Virginia, Georgia and South Carolina.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>It is not a listing service</h2>
    <p>
      Most of what is here is a <strong>parcel record</strong>. A parcel record says a property
      exists and describes it. It does not say the property is for sale, and it certainly does not
      mean its owner wants to hear from you. That is why the interface has two lanes that are never
      mixed: <em>On market / in distress</em>, where a dated and linked piece of evidence exists,
      and <em>Prospecting — not known to be for sale</em>, which ships collapsed and labelled in
      full everywhere it appears.
    </p>

    <h2>No owner names</h2>
    <p>
      Owner names are stripped before anything is published. The site shows only booleans —
      out-of-state owner, entity-owned — and an integer for years since the last recorded transfer.
      There is no name in this site, in the published payload, or in the repository, and a build
      gate re-checks the built output for one on every deploy rather than trusting that a type
      prevented it.
    </p>

    <h2>Unknown is not zero</h2>
    <p>
      A missing assessed value renders as <span class="unk">unknown</span>, never as $0. A missing
      acreage renders as <span class="unk">unknown</span>, never as 0 ac. In the score, an unknown
      signal is excluded from the denominator rather than scored zero — because "we could not see
      it" and "it is bad" are different facts, and flattening them would confidently rank the
      counties we cannot see below the counties we can, which is exactly backwards.
    </p>

    <h2>Coverage is stated, not implied</h2>
    <p>
      Every county carries a tier: <strong>rich</strong>, <strong>partial</strong>,
      <strong>thin</strong> or <strong>notices-only</strong>. An empty result in a rich county means
      we looked and found nothing. An empty result in a notices-only county means no parcel data
      source exists there and we cannot see it at all. The interface never renders those two the
      same way. <a href={withBase('/status/')}>The per-county table is here.</a>
    </p>

    <h2>The crawler</h2>
    <ul class="about__facts">
      <li><strong>User agent:</strong> <code>{site.crawlerUA}</code></li>
      <li><strong>Rate:</strong> one request at a time per host, with a delay between requests, and it honours <code>robots.txt</code> and <code>Crawl-delay</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>What it fetches:</strong> published parcel/assessment layers and public sale and foreclosure notices. Nothing behind a login, nothing behind a paywall, no form submissions.</li>
      <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> one person evaluating land to buy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to make it stop</h2>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        Email <a href={`mailto:${site.contact}`}>{site.contact}</a> or open an issue on the
        repository. Naming a host stops all fetching of it in the next commit — a <code>PAUSE</code>
        file in the repo stops <em>all</em> egress within one commit. You do not need to block
        anything or file anything for this to work, and asking is faster than either.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Search engines</h2>
    <p>
      This site sends <code>noindex, nofollow</code> on every page and <code>Disallow: /</code> in
      robots.txt. It has no SEO goal, and a search-result page for someone's tax foreclosure is a
      harm with no upside here. Be clear about the limit, though: that is hygiene, not access
      control. A public Pages site has none.
    </p>

    <h2>Do not act on this alone</h2>
    <p>
      Everything here is derived from a public record by an automated process, and every row links
      to its source so you can check it. Acreage marked GIS-derived is measured from a published
      polygon and is not a survey. Assessed values have a vintage and some counties reappraise on a
      cycle that will move them. Confirm against the deed, the survey, the plat and the county
      before spending money.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about__facts {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    max-width: 72ch;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 0.92em;
  }
  h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-l);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xs);
  }
</style>
